Sessions and locks
02-sessions-and-locks
The upper list is every session: its user number, the Linux process, when it signed on, what kind it is (user, phantom, API or SDNet), the login user, the account it is in, where it came from, and whether it is yours or a logout is pending. The lower list is every lock: the user, the file number and path, the type (FX and SX lock a whole file, RU is read and update, RL is read, WAIT is a session waiting for a lock), the record id, since when, and whether the holder is still signed on. Both are read again every three seconds and on F5, and the cursor stays on the same user or the same lock.
Tab changes the list. L logs the selected session out: SD signals its process, work it has not saved is lost and the locks it holds are released; the page shows the command and asks you to type the user number again. If the process is already gone, the entry is cleared instead and its locks are released, so L is also the cure for a dead session and for one that still shows (logout pending). The page does not offer it for your own session. U releases the selected lock with the unlock verb; the holder is not told. A waiting row is not a lock. I stops or allows new sessions with inhibit.login; you can always sign in as SDSYS to switch it off.
The page changes nothing by itself. Each key builds one command from values it has read and checked, shows it with what it does, and runs it only when you answer Run; Cancel is what Enter chooses. Each verb asks for the administrator itself, and because logout and unlock write no audit line of their own the page writes one for every command it ran.
